A["delta__N"] = {
"share": "The switch-on line is the price-war incentive constraint: without a tick a cartel is sustainable iff δ/(1−δ) ≥ (1−φ)(N−1), i.e. δ ≥ 1 − 1/N at φ = 0 (screens and tips lower effective patience to δ(1−ρ_T)), so N = 10 needs δ ≥ 0.9 and the δ = 0.5 row collapses from 0.373 at N = 2 to 0.000 at N = 10. By δ = 0.99 the constraint is slack almost everywhere and N barely matters (0.255–0.274): in tick-free markets every per-firm payoff and the fine (scaled to per-firm monopoly profit) shrink by the same 1/N, so N acts only through the incentive constraint. The high-δ decline itself is not a patience effect: in tick-free cells the N = 2 share stays at 0.511 from δ = 0.7 to 0.99, while in tick cells it drops from 0.345 to 0.128, the π_post = 0 artifact (a trigger without evidence destroys a tick-rent stream worth π^N/(1−δ)), which is also why N = 2 is the lowest column from δ = 0.9 up.",
"theta": "At low δ θ* tracks the same incentive frontier (N = 10: 0.000 at δ = 0.5, 0.011 at 0.7), so the peak moves right as N rises (N = 2 peaks at δ = 0.7 with 0.328, N = 10 at δ = 0.95 with 0.231). Past the peak θ* falls with patience, partly for a genuine reason, the external-risk regime: a patient cartel has more future rent to lose from a trigger and lowers its margin to reduce the screen hazard (in tick-free cells θ* among colluders falls from 0.741 at δ = 0.7 to 0.588 at δ = 0.99), and partly from the tick artifact. At δ = 0.99 θ* is nearly identical across N (0.166–0.177; tick-free 0.293–0.302), confirming that once the incentive constraint is slack N has no lever on the chosen margin."}

A["delta__phi"] = {
"share": "Preferenced flow is split equally whatever the price, so an undercut wins only the (1−φ) contestable part and the patience threshold falls to δ/(1−δ) ≥ (1−φ)(N−1); that is why φ matters most at δ = 0.5 (0.099 → 0.247) and hardly at all from δ = 0.9 up (0.360–0.373), where the constraint is already slack. In tick-free markets φ enters only through that constraint (prices, the screen and the fine do not depend on φ), and the column effect vanishes at high δ (tick-free shares 0.509–0.511 at δ = 0.99). The slight reversal at δ ≥ 0.95 (0.274 → 0.265 at δ = 0.99) comes from tick cells (0.156 → 0.142), likely because with N ≤ 3 more preferencing raises the static-Nash tick margin (j* rises from 1 to 2–3), shrinking the collusive range and enlarging the rent stream exposed to the π_post = 0 artifact.",
"theta": "Same incentive logic: at δ = 0.5 θ* more than triples with φ (0.046 → 0.167) because more cells pass the constraint and fewer have it capping θ below the preferred margin; in tick-free cells the contrast is starker (0.008 at φ = 0 vs 0.159 at φ = 0.6). From δ = 0.95 the columns converge (0.241–0.243, then 0.171–0.175) because the constraint no longer binds. The rise-then-fall in δ combines easier sustainability at moderate patience with margin restraint at high patience (external-risk regime) plus the tick artifact."}

A["delta__tick"] = {
"share": "The tick pulls two ways. At low patience it helps (δ = 0.5: 0.231 with a 0.25 tick vs 0.115 without): a one-tick undercut is a large proportional cut at narrow margins and the price war falls to s₀, below the tick-rent Nash margin, so cheating is less tempting; with no screen and no tip the δ = 0.5 share is 0.560 with a 0.25 tick vs 0.267 without. At high patience the tick columns fall (tick 0.25: 0.380 at δ = 0.9 to 0.188 at 0.99) while the no-tick column keeps rising to 0.510; this decline is the π_post = 0 artifact, because restricting to P(evidence) = 1, where every trigger ends in conviction, the tick-0.25 share is flat (0.290 at δ = 0.9, 0.293 at 0.99).",
"theta": "Tick-free θ* rises to 0.343 at δ = 0.95 and eases to 0.300 at 0.99 (genuine external-risk restraint), whereas with a 0.5 tick it peaks at 0.182 (δ = 0.8) and falls to 0.084. At P(evidence) = 1 the 0.25-tick column is nearly flat at high δ (0.222 → 0.202), so most of the high-δ collapse is the calibration, not economics. Coarser ticks lower θ* at every δ ≥ 0.8, plausibly because the cartel must move in whole-tick steps and cannot fine-tune a low-visibility margin."}

A["delta__slope"] = {
"share": "The screen is the main deterrent: raising the slope from 0 to 0.2 cuts the share by roughly two-thirds to four-fifths in every row (δ = 0.9: 0.670 → 0.169), and each slope column peaks at δ = 0.8–0.95, once patience has made the incentive constraint slack. The drop at δ = 0.99 (slope 0.025: 0.424 → 0.290 from δ = 0.95) comes from tick cells: in tick-free markets the sign of the collusion gain, π_c − ρ_E·F, does not involve δ, so once the constraint holds the share stops moving (0.570 vs 0.575 at slope 0.025). In tick markets every screen trigger without evidence destroys the π^N/(1−δ) tick-rent stream under π_post = 0, a loss that grows with δ.",
"theta": "With no screen the hazard does not depend on the margin, so colluders go straight to monopoly (in tick-free cells θ* equals the share, 0.792 at δ = 0.99), so slope 0 is the highest column in every row, peaking at 0.676 at δ = 0.95. With any screen θ* is interior and falls with patience past δ ≈ 0.9: tick-free θ* at slope 0.01 is 0.529 at δ = 0.95 and 0.464 at 0.99, the genuine external-risk effect, because each extra unit of markup raises the hazard of losing rents that last 1/(1−δ) rounds. Even a gentle screen bites hard: slope 0.01 cuts θ* at δ = 0.9 from 0.664 to 0.433."}

A["delta__rhoTip"] = {
"share": "A tip triggers an investigation whatever the margin, so pricing low cannot avoid it; it shortens expected life and, in the incentive constraint, lowers effective patience to δ(1−ρ_T), and a 5% tip halves the share at δ = 0.9 (0.479 → 0.243). In tick-free cells without tips the share saturates by δ = 0.9 (0.605), but with a 5% tip it keeps rising to 0.409 at δ = 0.99, so patience partly offsets the tip through the constraint. The aggregate fall at δ = 0.99 (no tip: 0.479 → 0.367) comes from tick cells (0.416 → 0.248), where each tip that finds no evidence wipes out the tick rents (π_post = 0 artifact); there the 5% tip leaves only 0.057.",
"theta": "Mean θ* falls with the tip (δ = 0.9: 0.332 → 0.182), but that is a participation effect: among colluding cells the tip raises θ* (0.693 → 0.749 at δ = 0.9), as the spec predicts, because a cartel that may be caught anyway gains less from staying inconspicuous. The effect is largest at δ = 0.99 (0.589 → 0.733 given participation), where screen-driven restraint is otherwise strongest."}

A["delta__pEvidence"] = {
"share": "In tick-free markets P(evidence) matters only through the expected fine P(evidence)×F, so the share falls in every row (δ = 0.99: 0.705 → 0.360). The aggregate δ = 0.99 row instead dips and recovers (0.311, 0.253, 0.227, 0.286) because tick cells there rise from 0.114 to 0.249 as P(evidence) goes from 0.1 to 1.0: under π_post = 0 a trigger without evidence is worse than a conviction (firms lose all tick rents instead of paying F and returning to π^N), and the gap grows with the horizon. That upturn is a calibration artifact, not a deterrence result.",
"theta": "θ* falls with P(evidence) because the marginal expected penalty of a higher margin is (∂P(trigger)/∂θ)×P(evidence)×F; the largest fall is at δ = 0.8 (0.331 → 0.178). The U-shape at δ = 0.99 (0.193 → 0.149 → 0.189) is the same tick artifact: tick-free θ* falls monotonically there (0.402 → 0.220)."}

A["delta__fineRatio"] = {
"share": "fineRatio is F as a multiple of one round of full-monopoly profit per firm (π_c at θ = 1); the fine bites only where a screen or tip can trigger, and the share falls steadily (δ = 0.9: 0.848 with no fine to 0.059 at 500×). Patience raises the share in every fine column up to δ = 0.9 by easing the incentive constraint; beyond that, in tick-free markets participation does not depend on δ (the gain has the sign of π_c − ρ_E·F), so the tick-free share at zero fine is 1.000 at both δ = 0.95 and 0.99. The δ = 0.99 drop at low fines (zero fine: 0.848 at δ = 0.9 to 0.580) comes from tick cells (0.805 → 0.370), the π_post = 0 artifact.",
"theta": "θ* falls with the fine in every row (δ = 0.9: 0.591 → 0.052) because the marginal penalty of extra markup, slope × P(evidence) × F, scales with F. Beyond the incentive constraint the two barely interact: every fine column peaks at δ = 0.9 (tied with 0.95 at 250×), and at 500× the rows converge (0.017–0.052)."}

A["delta__sBarRatio"] = {
"share": "Two effects of a wider demand range s̄/s₀ are averaged here. In tick-free markets s̄/s₀ acts only as a multiplier on the screen (the monopoly markup is (s̄/s₀ − 1)/2 in absolute units and the screen measures absolute markup), so the share falls monotonically (δ = 0.99: 0.642 → 0.390). In tick markets a narrow range leaves few or no tick steps above the Nash margin (with a 0.5 tick and s̄/s₀ = 2 the collusive range is empty), so the tick share rises from 0.064 to 0.184 at δ = 0.99. The inverted U peaking at s̄/s₀ = 5 (0.408 at δ = 0.9) is the sum of the two; patience lifts every column through the incentive constraint, and the δ = 0.99 dip again comes from tick cells.",
"theta": "θ* peaks at s̄/s₀ = 3 in every row (e.g. 0.317 at δ = 0.9). Tick-free θ* falls monotonically with s̄/s₀ (δ = 0.9: 0.480 → 0.220) because the same θ is a larger absolute markup and therefore a higher screen hazard. At s̄/s₀ = 2, 0.5-tick cells cannot collude at all and 0.25-tick cells have a single step (θ* is 0 or 1), which pulls that column down."}

A["N__phi"] = {
"share": "Preferencing helps most where cheating temptation is greatest: at N = 10 the share rises from 0.143 to 0.258 with φ, while at N = 2 it is flat or slightly negative (0.361 → 0.339). Since the threshold is (1−φ)(N−1) ≤ δ/(1−δ), φ matters only where N makes the constraint bind; the share of cells where any collusion is sustainable at N = 10 rises from 0.388 to 0.679. At N = 2 two things offset: in tick-free cells φ still helps (0.459 → 0.511), but in tick cells it hurts (0.312 → 0.253), likely because with two firms more preferencing raises the static-Nash tick margin (j* from 1 to 2–3), shrinking the collusive range and enlarging the π^N stream exposed to the π_post = 0 artifact.",
"theta": "θ* fans out the same way: flat at N = 2 (0.264–0.267) and nearly doubling at N = 10 (0.080 → 0.159). In tick-free markets N and φ enter only through the incentive constraint, so φ's gain comes from relaxing it: more cells collude and fewer are capped below their preferred margin (the share of cells in the constraint-binding interior regime falls from 0.066 at φ = 0 to 0.046 at φ = 0.6)."}

A["N__tick"] = {
"share": "A tick lowers the share at every N but by less at large N (N = 2: 0.494 → 0.216; N = 10: 0.247 → 0.129), because it has an offsetting benefit that matters only where the incentive constraint binds: with a 0.25 tick a one-tick undercut is a large proportional cut and the price war drops below the tick-rent margin, so the share of N = 10 cells where some collusion is sustainable rises from 0.465 to 0.628. At N = 2, where the constraint rarely binds, only the costs show: fewer price steps, a minimum markup of one full tick for the screen to see, and the π_post = 0 artifact. With P(evidence) = 1 (artifact off), a 0.25 tick even nudges the share up at N = 10 (0.177 → 0.183) while still cutting it at N = 2 (0.349 → 0.279).",
"theta": "θ* falls with the tick at every N (N = 2: 0.355 → 0.176; N = 10: 0.145 → 0.079), by about half in each case, slightly less at N = 10 (−46% vs −50%), consistent with the tick's easing of the incentive constraint there. The fall reflects whole-tick steps, the screen seeing at least one tick of markup, and the tick artifact."}

A["N__slope"] = {
"share": "N and the screen work through different channels, so their effects nearly multiply: N raises cheating temptation, the screen raises the hazard and expected penalty. In tick-free markets the screen does not depend on N (it sees the absolute markup) and per-firm payoffs and fines all scale with 1/N, so N acts only through the incentive constraint, which is why N = 2 and 3 are almost identical (0.652 vs 0.637 at slope 0). The N penalty grows with the slope (N = 10 to N = 2 ratio 0.59 at slope 0, 0.48 at 0.2) because the screen hazard also lowers effective patience δ(1−ρ_T) in the constraint: the share of N = 10 cells where collusion is sustainable falls from 0.541 to 0.481 across slopes, while N = 2 stays at 0.915–0.921.",
"theta": "θ* falls steeply with the slope for every N (N = 2: 0.650 → 0.094), since each extra unit of markup raises the screen hazard; without a screen colluders choose monopoly (θ* among N = 2 colluders is 0.998 at slope 0). Among colluders, large N cuts θ* more under steep screens (0.534 at N = 2 vs 0.269 at N = 10, slope 0.2): the screen hazard rises with θ, so the incentive constraint binds at high margins and caps θ* where cheating temptation is high (the internal-risk regime)."}

A["N__rhoTip"] = {
"share": "A tip ends the cartel whatever the margin, so it cuts effective patience to δ(1−ρ_T), which bites hardest where the incentive constraint is tight: the 5% tip lowers the share by 46% at N = 2 (0.452 → 0.243) but 60% at N = 10 (0.272 → 0.108). The share of N = 10 cells where collusion is sustainable falls from 0.546 to 0.479 with the tip, while N = 2 barely moves (0.917 → 0.915). The rest of the tip effect, expected fines and shorter tenure, does not depend on N.",
"theta": "θ* falls with the tip (N = 2: 0.326 → 0.198; N = 10: 0.157 → 0.065), mostly because fewer cells collude; given participation a tip raises the chosen margin (see the δ × ρ_tip split). The proportional fall is larger at N = 10 (−59% vs −39%) for the same incentive-constraint reason."}

