A["slope__rhoTip"] = {
"share": "The two triggers are substitutes, since P(trigger) = 1 − (1 − ρ_screen)(1 − ρ_tip): a tip does the most damage where there is no screen (slope 0: 0.787 → 0.310) and little where the screen already dominates (slope 0.2: 0.166 → 0.117). The slope-0, no-tip cell is the no-hazard case: nothing can trigger, the fine is never paid, and 0.787 is simply the share of cells where collusion is sustainable.",
"theta": "Given participation, a tip raises the chosen margin wherever there is a screen (tick-free colluders at slope 0.01: 0.703 → 0.897), because a cartel that may be caught by a tip anyway gains less from hiding; mean θ* still falls with the tip because fewer cells collude. At slope 0 the hazard does not depend on θ, so tick-free colluders all choose monopoly (θ* given participation = 1.000), and θ* drops from 0.748 to 0.293 only through participation."}

A["slope__pEvidence"] = {
"share": "P(evidence) prices each trigger and the screen generates triggers, so the two compound: the proportional fall from P(evidence) 0.1 to 1.0 is 24% at slope 0 (0.636 → 0.485), where only tips trigger, and 46% at slope 0.05 (0.384 → 0.207). The upturn at P(evidence) = 1 for slopes ≥ 0.15 (slope 0.2: 0.109 → 0.115) is the tick artifact: tick-free shares fall monotonically (slope 0.2: 0.385 → 0.141), while tick cells rise between P(evidence) 0.6 and 1.0 (0.075 → 0.101).",
"theta": "θ* falls with P(evidence) at every slope (0.602 → 0.463 at slope 0; 0.086 → 0.054 at 0.2). The small upturns at P(evidence) = 1 for slopes ≥ 0.15 (0.061 → 0.065 at 0.15; 0.048 → 0.054 at 0.2) are the same artifact."}

A["slope__fineRatio"] = {
"share": "This is the sharpest interaction in the grid. A markup m earns rent of roughly m × Q/N but carries expected penalty slope × m × P(evidence) × F, so in tick-free cells with no tip collusion pays at small m iff slope × P(evidence) × fineRatio × (s̄/s₀ − 1) < 4 (verified in 98.9% of such cells; the rest are knife-edge cells with θ* < 0.001), and the share there depends only on the product slope × fine (slope 0.01 with fine 500, 0.1 with 50 and 0.2 with 25 all give 0.272). With no screen the fine works only through tips (0.741 → 0.307), and with slope ≥ 0.1 and fine ≥ 250 collusion all but disappears (≤ 0.004).",
"theta": "Without a screen even a 500× fine leaves θ* at 0.293, because most colluders there are hazard-free cells that go to monopoly, while at slope 0.2 θ* is 0.000 from fine 250. At zero fine the screen alone lowers θ* from 0.697 to 0.263: the cartel trades margin for longevity even with no penalty, because a trigger ends the rents."}

A["slope__sBarRatio"] = {
"share": "In tick-free markets s̄/s₀ acts purely as a multiplier on the screen: the monopoly markup is (s̄/s₀ − 1)/2 in absolute units and the screen measures absolute markup, so the share depends only on slope × (s̄/s₀ − 1) (slope 0.1 at s̄/s₀ = 2, 0.05 at 3 and 0.025 at 5 all give 0.419). So with no screen s̄/s₀ matters only through tick room (0.343 → 0.643, all from tick cells; the tick-free share is 0.593 at every s̄/s₀), while with a screen a wider range hurts.",
"theta": "θ* scales the same way (tick-free θ* is 0.282 for slope 0.1 at s̄/s₀ = 2, 0.05 at 3 and 0.025 at 5), so a screen in a market with wide demand range acts like a steeper screen in a narrow one. At slope 0 θ* rises with s̄/s₀ (0.343 → 0.597) from tick room alone; at slope 0.2 it falls from 0.100 to 0.024."}

A["rhoTip__pEvidence"] = {
"share": "Both raise the per-round expected penalty P(trigger) × P(evidence) × F, and they compound: raising P(evidence) from 0.1 to 1.0 lowers the share by 34% with no tip (0.482 → 0.318) but 48% with a 5% tip (0.282 → 0.146), because the tip makes a trigger possible at every margin, so conviction risk bites at every θ, not only at high markups. The fall from 0.6 to 1.0 at a 5% tip (0.158 → 0.146) is weaker than the tick-free fall (0.225 → 0.172) because tick cells partly reverse under the π_post = 0 artifact.",
"theta": "θ* falls with both (0.309 at no tip and P(evidence) 0.1; 0.109 at 5% and 1.0). On average the tip lowers θ* even though, given participation, it raises the margin; P(evidence) unambiguously lowers it because it multiplies the marginal penalty of extra markup."}

A["rhoTip__fineRatio"] = {
"share": "The tip creates an unavoidable penalty floor: whatever the margin, the expected fine per round is at least ρ_tip × P(evidence) × F, while the best possible per-round profit is monopoly profit π_c(1) = F/fineRatio. So collusion is impossible once ρ_tip × P(evidence) × fineRatio > 1 at every P(evidence) level, which is why a 5% tip gives exactly 0.000 at fines 250 and 500 (0.05 × 0.1 × 250 = 1.25). With no tip, the 500× share still holds at 0.125, largely cells with no screen, where nothing triggers and the fine is never paid.",
"theta": "θ* follows (5% tip: 0.425 → 0.000; no tip: 0.465 → 0.104). At zero fine the tip barely changes θ* (0.465 vs 0.425), likely because without a fine a tip only shortens the cartel's life, which costs some participation but raises the margin of those that stay."}

A["rhoTip__sBarRatio"] = {
"share": "The tip shifts the curve down at every s̄/s₀ (s̄/s₀ = 3: 0.433 → 0.196), proportionally more at narrow ranges (−58% at 2, −41% at 12), likely because there the screen sees only a small absolute markup, so the tip is the main trigger. In tick-free cells the share falls with s̄/s₀ at every tip level (no tip: 0.614 → 0.346) by screen scaling; the inverted U in the full table comes from tick cells needing room above the tick-rent margin.",
"theta": "θ* peaks at s̄/s₀ = 3 at every tip level (0.323, 0.259, 0.161). Beyond that greater visibility of the markup to the screen lowers it, and the tip lowers it throughout by cutting participation."}

A["pEvidence__fineRatio"] = {
"share": "In tick-free markets these two enter only as the product P(evidence) × F, the expected fine per trigger: the share at P(evidence) 0.1 with fine 125 equals that at 1.0 with fine 12.5 (0.380), and 0.3 × 25 equals 0.6 × 12.5 (0.493). The full table breaks that symmetry because of tick cells: at zero fine collusion rises with P(evidence) (0.617 → 0.767) although nothing is fined, which is the π_post = 0 artifact (tick-free share 0.741 in every row at zero fine). The floor near 0.033 at P(evidence) = 1 and fines ≥ 250 is cells with no screen and no tip, where no trigger ever happens.",
"theta": "θ* obeys the same product rule in tick-free cells (0.264 at 0.1 × 125 and at 1.0 × 12.5). At zero fine θ* rises with P(evidence) (0.406 → 0.521), the tick artifact again; once the fine is 12.5 or more, more evidence lowers θ* sharply (fine 25: 0.330 → 0.103)."}

A["pEvidence__sBarRatio"] = {
"share": "P(evidence) shifts the curve down at every s̄/s₀ (s̄/s₀ = 5: 0.439 → 0.259) and the inverted U survives (peak at 5 for P(evidence) ≤ 0.6, at 3 for 1.0). In tick-free cells the share falls monotonically with s̄/s₀ at every P(evidence) (0.1: 0.616 → 0.427), because the expected fine of a small markup, relative to its rent, scales with slope × P(evidence) × fineRatio × (s̄/s₀ − 1); the rise at the narrow end is tick room.",
"theta": "θ* peaks at s̄/s₀ = 3 for every P(evidence) and falls with P(evidence) everywhere (s̄/s₀ = 3: 0.315 → 0.210). Tick-free θ* falls monotonically in both directions (0.486 at P(evidence) 0.1, s̄/s₀ = 2; 0.119 at 1.0, 12)."}

A["fineRatio__sBarRatio"] = {
"share": "At zero fine the share rises with s̄/s₀ (0.443 → 0.763) only because tick cells need room above the tick-rent margin; tick-free cells are at 0.741 for every s̄/s₀. With a fine the pattern turns down past s̄/s₀ = 3–5 because fineRatio is scaled to monopoly profit, which grows with the demand range faster than the rent from a small markup; relative to that rent the fine grows in proportion to (s̄/s₀ − 1), so tick-free shares fall monotonically (fine 25: 0.606 → 0.255). By 500× the rows are flat near 0.05, mostly hazard-free cells.",
"theta": "θ* peaks at s̄/s₀ = 3 at every fine level (0.553 at zero fine) and falls beyond it; tick-free θ* falls monotonically even at zero fine (0.619 → 0.375), because the screen sees absolute markup, so the same θ is more conspicuous in a wide market. At high fines the columns converge (0.037–0.045 at 500×)."}
